# build: non gradle GUI  build

make
termux-share -a view app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk


# rationale 

with some effort and testing in the cloud to save band width you can revert any project to this exact version 

it's the only sensible way to build on device . multiple versions will require a hole in the wall in other words cloud rig

the benefit is to work offline if you rarely have a connection 

you can simulate off line mode with 

gradle assembleDebug --offline


# bounty gradle time machine : cradle 

i am looking for a tool that turn back the clock on gradle project to specify version automatically always struggle for hours to avoid the gigantic gradle downloads 

call it CRADLE or something back to the cradle . turn a 8.10 platform 35 project back to 8.1 33. voila it just works!

cradle --gradle 8.1 --adk 33 --plugin 8.0

success! you can now build it with what YOU have on hand. man tar vad man har - Swedish expression 

was a gigantic effort to find this and build it in termux. every gradle update is testing my patience on a poor connect long way from home . i end up spending more time to restore the gradle version than actual coding 


# build this project

curl -s "https://get.sdkman.io" | bash
source ~/.sdkman/bin/sdkman-init.sh
sdk install gradle 8.1.1

vim ~/.gradle/gradle.properties
android.aapt2FromMavenOverride=/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/aapt2

and it will run out if memory over and over you have to use earlyoom and consider these settings that make it much slower

org.gradle.parallel=false
org.gradle.daemon=false
org.gradle.jvmargs=-Xmx256m

either of these files should work for the platform 

sdkmanager --install   "platforms;android-33" 

https://github.com/AndroidIDEOfficial/androidide-tools/releases/download/sdk/android-sdk.tar.xz

this file will NOT work it's not for ARM

sdkmanager --install    "build-tools;34.0.4"

this is the arm build

https://github.com/AndroidIDEOfficial/androidide-tools/releases/download/v34.0.4/build-tools-34.0.4-aarch64.tar.xz

and finally 

gradle assembleDebug


I only use this version in termux because java and android expect infinite storage and network capacity . one single version is already too big 

the gain from the massive waste of space is reliable old apps. don't know why they care so desperately about it. on the opposite end is Haskell that is nearly impossible to even use five years old but has a small foot print 

that gradle want every minor version in a separate gigantic file is questioning the sanity of the java community. i hapen to love it but hate the downloads and the endless fight to restore the old versions in the settings 


